-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Many-to-One Mapping:** The perspective projection causes a many-to-one mapping from voxels to pixels, complicating control flow and requiring extra time to compute resampling weights [S2].
-- **Performance:** It is more expensive than parallel projection due to the additional resampling and complex control flow [S2, S3].
-- **Resolution Trade-off:** The scaling operation produces an intermediate image; its resolution can be chosen to provide accurate or coarser rendering, allowing for quality reduction during interaction [S3].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Diverging Rays:** Perspective rays diverge, making uniform sampling difficult. The scale factor handles this, but if not implemented correctly, artifacts may appear [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The many-to-one mapping from voxels to pixels complicates control flow and requires extra time for resampling weight computation, making it slower than parallel Shear-Warp [S2, S3].
+*   **Resolution Choice:** The resolution of the intermediate image can be chosen to trade off accuracy for speed, allowing for coarser rendering during interaction [S3].
 
